TEXT 1	; Closeup on the central fortress
Fortress N73A3 is an important asset to the empire, containing records
which go back nearly 2000 years. 

In these records we find references to four large sensor complexes which 
have been scanning this part of the galaxy for millennia. These planetoids, 
at [C2:42], [C2:43], [C2:44], and [C2:45], each have unique data pertaining
to activities of interest in our quadrant.  Our strategists say they could 
be of vital importance to Fleet Intelligence. 
ENDTEXT

TEXT 2	; Fortress teaser text
This Fortress is said to command interesting information regarding high-
gain sensor arrays in this region.  Fleet Intelligence recommends that we 
conquer the base, to discover what we can about this part of the galaxy.

Also, control of this base would give us strong leverage in gaining control
of the central nebula.  This would probably be our most important tool in 
subjugating other empires.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 3	; Artificial world 2, displaying Constructions
This planet-sized sensor array and production facility has been monitoring
large-scale movement of resources. Based on these data we are convinced that
large space-based objects are being constructed at the following locations:

[C1:50]     [C1:49]     [C1:48]     [C1:47]     [C1:46]     [C1:45]
[C1:44]     [C1:43]     [C1:42]     [C1:41]     [C1:40]     [C1:39]
[C1:38]     [C1:37]     [C1:36]     [C1:35]     [C1:34]     [C1:33]
[C1:32]     [C1:31]     [C1:30]     [C1:29]     [C1:28]     [C1:27]
ENDTEXT

TEXT 4	; Artificial world 1, displaying Capitals
This planet-sized sensor array and production facility has been monitoring
fleet movements for centuries. Based on statistical studies of the data, we
have determined that the following planets are almost certainly of important
political or military significance:

[C2:1]        [C2:9]        [C2:13]        [C2:16]
[C2:34]        [C2:36]        [C2:37]        [C2:38]
ENDTEXT

TEXT 5	; Artificial world 4, displaying Starbases
This planet-sized sensor array and production facility has been monitoring
gravimetric signatures from all over the quadrant.  Based on these data, we
believe that man-made, planet-sized bases exist at the following locations:

[C3:1]     [C3:2]     [C3:3]     [C3:4]     [C3:100]     [C3:99]
[C3:98]     [C3:97]     [C3:96]     [C3:95]     [C3:94]     [C3:93]
[C3:92]     [C3:91]     [C3:90]     [C3:89]     [C3:88]     [C3:87]
[C3:86]     [C3:85]     [C3:84]     [C3:83]     [C3:82]     [C3:81]
ENDTEXT

TEXT 6	; Artificial world 3, displaying Stargates
This planet-sized sensor array and production facility has been monitoring
transspace distortions in our quadrant for 1600 years. From their baseline
observations, they have concluded that the following sectors contain space-
distorting fields known as StarGates, Warp Links, or Jumpspace Disruptors:

[C4:50]     [C4:49]     [C4:48]     [C4:47]     [C4:46]     [C4:45]
[C4:44]     [C4:43]     [C4:42]     [C4:41]     [C4:40]     [C4:39]
[C4:38]     [C4:37]     [C4:36]     [C4:35]     [C4:34]     [C4:33]
ENDTEXT

TEXT 7	; Artificial world teaser text
This artificial planet is emitting high-gain paraspace transmissions on
Garman-Slot intervals. This suggests that it may contain specialized sensor 
arrays which are far superior to our own. Fleet Intelligence suggests we 
pursue the conquest of this planet, at Your Majesty's convenience.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 8	; Instructions for player 1
The gate at [C4:50] is the key to your success. Don't be tempted to send
so many war fleets from that you leave it undefended. If you can keep it 
until the endgame, it will lead your empire to total supremacy and victory. 
Lose it too early, and your best strategy will be to hide in the nebula 
until you can build up a strong fleet. 

The sensor base at [C2:45] will reveal the location of your stargate to any
empire who can conquer it. If you can't secure the base for yourself, at 
least keep others from gaining access to its secrets.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 9	; Instructions for player 2
Your arena is Jumpspace. In it, you stand a chance of victory; outside it,
in the molasses of Warpspace, your planets will slowly starve in isolation.

Conservation is faster than production - don't sacrifice your jumpfleets 
lightly. If you squander your starting resources, you will soon find that
you cannot stand your ground. If you maintain and build your fleets, you 
will be able to strike swiftly and strongly, the nebula can shield the 
location of your critical resources, and your expansion will be sure and 
resolute.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 10	; Instructions for player 3
You build on a core of high technology, but to compete with the high pro-
duction of other empires, you must build up a corpus of planets which can 
use your technology to create the machines that will conquer the galaxy. 

Use your advanced weaponry to overwhelm your targets. The threat of a 
heavy Ninja invasion can scare even advanced worlds into early submission,
giving you fewer casualties and stronger conquered worlds. Your empire can
only succeed on the premise that Knowledge is Power. Scout out the nebula 
early on, and keep an eye on tempting targets and threats alike.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 11	; Instructions for player 4
"Our Union is Our Strength." Each planet in your consortium is important
to the health of the whole, and none should be considered useless. 

Your planets seem like easy prey to your more advanced neighbors, and only 
adamant defense can convince them otherwise. Your universities will thrust
your technology forward quickly, and most of your planets will come along
for the ride. Keep them together, pool their resources, and direct their
industry in productive paths, and you will emerge from your corner of the 
galaxy like a lion among lambs, unheeded until you were too late to stop.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 12	; Information about empire 1
Even the oldest records on this planet cannot say who first built the ex-
tensive network of soil-lined pods that orbit this gargantuan planet, but
all agree that the ancient spaceship production facilities are still among
the most efficient in the quadrant. More importantly to us, however, is 
the intelligence report that this empire may control a stargate somewhere
in the nebula. Doubtless constructed by the First Great Empire, it would
be capable of projecting a fleet to anywhere in the galaxy in a matter of
days. If this is true, the gate could be a threat to our own prosperity.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 13 ; Information about empire 2
A relative newcomer to space-faring culture, this planet has a history of
violent nations whose sole ambition was to steal the welfare of others.

After a lucky capture of a jumpfleet in this sector, this planet declared
itself a sovereign empire and proceeded to tyrannize the neighboring
systems with jumpfleets patterned after those it had captured. Now a grow-
ing number of planets fly the spotted flag of this empire, and produce
the jumpships needed to magnify their independence - and their strength.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 14	; Information about empire 3
This empire is one of the oldest in the quadrant, second only to the 
fabled 'Sartid,' if they truly exist. The three advanced planets of this 
empire have historically been peaceful, but the recent bloody ascent to 
the throne by a new emperor may just be a taste of violence and aggres-
sion to come.

The low population of this empire has always hampered production, even in
the face of their advanced technology, but if they conquer enough planets
to increase their production significantly ... we will have to be cautious.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 15	; Information about empire 4
The Warp Consortium was a loose incorporation of low-tech planets whose 
greatest strength (and indeed, almost their only asset) was their unity in
such a large group. Consisting of nearly 20 planets when they declared
themselves a legal Empire, they have a strong production base and economic
resiliency which gives them a tactical advantage.

Their current independence owes in part to their control of starbases which
give them access to various ancient jumpfleet deterrents near their empire.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 16 ; Information about empire 5
This is a relatively small empire which has not yet learned the basics of
interplanetary etiquette. Their jumpfleets cruise through the nebula, with
the sole intent to raid and plunder.  A nuisance at best, this empire 
should be dealt with swiftly and decisively.

Fleet Command suggests that any effort put into negotiations with this
empire would most likely be wasted.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 17	; Information about empires 6 and 7
This empire has no real claim to uniqueness in this quadrant.  Ruled by a
short-sighted emperor with no real tactical awareness, it likely poses a 
significant threat only to some of our outlying worlds. That said, if we
leave any of our planets undefended, it is likely that they would be 
snatched up by this empire. Remember, Your Highness, that conservation of
our resources will be easier than re-conquering them.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 18 ; Information about empire 8
This is the capital of the Sartid empire, about which many fairy tales
are told. While any of its planets would be great assets to our empire,
Fleet Command suggests that we not pursue them unless we are in an un-
assailable position. The historic tactical and political maneuvers of 
the Sartids are the stuff of legend, and it is not advisable to provoke 
their anger. The last few centuries have been among the most peaceful in 
their history; it would be a shame to change that on our account.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 19 ; More background information for player 1
This small paradise planet was added to the empire recently after one of
our conquest fleets was transported through a wormhole to this remote
part of the galaxy. The wormhole was likely a residual transspace dis-
tortion caused by the nearby derelict stargate. Fortunately, the fleet 
was able to commandeer the gate for a return voyage, or we would never 
have known of their fate.

Indeed, their accident shapes our future. This remote colony - already 
expanding - has given us the hope of excellence in the galaxy.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 20 ; More background information for player 2
Uncanny good fortune seems to accompany our empire through the black 
void. Years of clan warfare ended when we captured a single jumpship
in our home sector. We were able, with some difficulty, to reproduce 
that first jumpship and begin the large corpus of ships we now control.

Then, nearly a decade later, we discovered this huge relic from another 
age. Before, we blindly groped in the darkness for our growth. Now this 
outpost is our Eye in the Nebula.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 21 ; More background information for player 3
Before your ascendance to the throne, this empire had not changed one
whit in almost 90 years. This Ninja base is the most recent development.

Like the shock troops that are trained here, our empire is lean, clear-
headed, and technologically advanced. Like these Ninjas, we long to
stretch our long-constricted limbs and look to the distant stars again.

We hunger for action.
ENDTEXT

TEXT 22 ; More background information for player 4
Artifacts from this planet were crucial in unraveling the language of 
the lost civilization that went before us, and also made known to us
the existence of the deep-space starbases nearby. And after the heroic
efforts to find those bases in the vastness of space were successful, 
this planet once again was the focal point of our efforts to resurrect
their computers and sensor systems, which led, later, to the discovery 
and control of the SRMs and jumpspace disrupter within our space.

Truly, this planet is the academic heart of the Consortium.
ENDTEXT

ENDDESCRIPTION

